1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Replica sets: oplog, lag, and elections. One ordered change history, three members, and no instant failover.

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,700
A replica set: members that maintain the same logical dataset.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,100
Under normal operation, only the primary (A) accepts writes.

4
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,400
A applies the insert and records it as entry #1 in its oplog: an ordered log of data changes.

5
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:19,700
Secondaries fetch entries from that history and apply them to their own copies.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,400
Replication is asynchronous. More writes arrive; B keeps up, C falls behind.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,800
C is lagging. Its data is older, which is not the same as lost: it is still catching up.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:31,233
A keeps only a bounded window of recent entries. Watch the oldest ones drop off.

9
00:00:31,733 --> 00:00:34,733
C fell behind the retained history. Incremental catch-up is no longer enough: C needs resynchronization.

10
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:38,333
A bounded replication log is not an archive of every earlier state.

11
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:42,633
Start again with all three members caught up. Now A fails.

12
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:46,333
B and C stop hearing from A. Noticing the failure takes time.

13
00:00:46,833 --> 00:00:50,033
B and C can still talk. Two of three votes is a majority, so an eligible member can be elected.

14
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:53,733
Here B becomes the new primary (one possible outcome).

15
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:55,733
The driver discovers the new topology and sends writes to B.

16
00:00:56,233 --> 00:01:00,133
Until then, some operations fail or wait. Don't promise a fixed number of seconds.

17
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:02,633
Different failure: A is lost AND B and C cannot reach each other.

18
00:01:03,133 --> 00:01:06,133
Each survivor has one vote out of three. Neither can become primary.

19
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:09,633
Why so strict? If isolated members promoted themselves, two primaries could accept conflicting writes.

20
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:12,633
Atlas Free runs a fixed three-node replica set and does not offer failover testing. Reason about it here.

21
00:01:14,233 --> 00:01:24,233
Remember: The primary accepts writes and records them in its oplog. Secondaries copy and apply entries asynchronously: they can lag. The oplog is bounded: it is not an archive of old states. A new primary needs a voting majority (2 of 3 here). Detection, election, and reconnection take time.

